import Lean

/-!
# Declaration manifest for the `alt`, `classical_upstream` and automata packages

Walks the compiled environment and records, for every declaration whose defining
module belongs to the selected package, the declaration's full name and that module.

    lake build                                          # the manifest reads .olean files
    lake env lean --run DeclManifest.lean alt [OUT]       # default OUT: ../scripts/alt-decls.txt
    lake env lean --run DeclManifest.lean classical [OUT] # default OUT: ../scripts/classical-decls.txt
    lake env lean --run DeclManifest.lean automata [OUT]  # default OUT: ../scripts/automata-decls.txt

The argument selects the package: `alt` is the module `ALT` and everything under it;
`classical` is everything under the eight library roots of `classical_upstream`
(`classicalRoots` below); `automata` is the modules `Nonautonomous` and `ECA` and
everything under them (package `elementary_cellular_automata`).  One process serves one package,
because of the single
extension-loading import per process (next section).  The named-condition test of the
hypothesis section recognizes a Prop-valued definition of ANY of the three packages as "our own",
so a row does not depend on which package's run produced it.

This file is deliberately NOT part of the `ALT` library (it lives at the
workspace root, outside the `ALT.*` glob), so it is never imported by the
development it measures.

## One environment, one extension-loading import

The counting rule needs environment *extensions* (`isStructure`,
`isAuxRecursor`, `isNoConfusion` read tables that only `loadExts := true`
populates — without them they answer `false` to everything, so the filter would
silently stop filtering instead of failing).  Lean permits that once per
process: `importModules (loadExts := true)` clears the initializers flag it
consumed, so a second such import in the same process is refused.

That interlock used to force a two-process design here, because the modules
built against `Foundation` could not be co-imported with the Mathlib-side
umbrella (both dependencies declared a root `Matrix.map`, and importing two
different declarations under one name is an import-time error, not a
name-resolution one).  Upstream renamed theirs, so the umbrella `ALT` now
imports every module of the development and a single environment over that one
root sees the whole package.  One process, one extension-loading import.

## The counting rule

The filter below IS the counting rule: whatever survives it is what this
manifest calls a "declaration", and any figure quoted from the manifest means
exactly that.  A constant defined in a package module is counted unless it is

* internal — `Name.isInternal` (macro scopes and `_`-prefixed components:
  `…._cstage1`, `…._sunfold`, `…._proof_1`, …).  A `private` declaration is
  *authored*, not generated, so it is counted, under the source-level name its
  `_private.…` mangling hides (`privateToUserName?`);
* a constructor or a recursor — `ConstantInfo.ctorInfo` / `.recInfo`
  (`Foo.mk`, `Foo.rec`);
* an auxiliary recursor or no-confusion helper — `isAuxRecursor` /
  `isNoConfusion` (`Foo.recOn`, `Foo.casesOn`, `Foo.brecOn`, `Foo.below`, …);
* a structure projection — a name `S.f` where `S` is a structure with field `f`
  (including parent projections such as `S.toFoo`);
* a helper Lean reserves for an inductive or a constructor — a name `T.s` whose
  parent `T` is an inductive/constructor and whose last component `s` is one of
  `inductiveHelpers` below (`Foo.below`, `Foo.mk.injEq`, …).  Some of these are
  generated lazily and never reach the aux-recursor table, so the name test
  backs up the structural one;
* a helper generated *for* a definition — a name `d.s` whose parent `d` is a
  declaration and whose last component `s` is one of `definitionHelpers` below,
  or one of the numbered families `eq_1, eq_2, …` (equation lemmas), `match_1`,
  `match_1_1`, `match_1.splitter`, … (matchers and their splitters), `proof_1, …`
  (extracted proof terms).

Every test is structural where the environment knows the answer, and every
name-shaped test carries a witness — the parent it hangs off must really exist,
and for the inductive helpers must really be an inductive.  A rule that guesses
from names alone drops authored declarations: `ALT.RecursorAlgebra` authors a
`def rec`, and a blanket "drop anything called `rec`" ate it.

Everything else is counted: `def`, `abbrev`, `theorem`, `instance`, `structure`,
`inductive` — one constant per declaration written in the source — plus the
instances a `deriving` clause asks for.  An anonymous or `local instance` counts
under the name Lean gives it.  Constructors, projections and recursors are
*generated by* a written declaration and are therefore not counted separately: a
`structure` with five fields counts once, as a reader of the source counts it.

Grepping the sources for declaration keywords does *not* reproduce this count,
in either direction: with one sentence per line, docstring prose beginning
"theorem …" or "structure map is …" is counted as a declaration, while a
`noncomputable local instance` or an `@[simp] theorem` is missed.

## The field section

A structure's field projections are *citable* — `ParamNNO.orbit_injective` is a
name a paper may legitimately quote — but they are not *counted*: a `structure`
counts once no matter how many fields it has, as a reader of the source counts
it.  The two facts are not in tension, they are about different questions, and
the manifest answers both: the declaration lines are what the counts count, and
a trailing `field <module> <name>` section lists exactly the projections the
counting rule excludes.  Consumers that check citations read both sections;
consumers that quote a figure read the counts.  Private parents get the same
source-level-name treatment as everywhere else (`privateToUserName?`).

## The private section

`private` hides a declaration outside its own file, but it is still a
*citable* source-level name — `userName` already un-mangles it for identity,
so a `private` declaration is counted exactly like a public one (the counting
rule above tests nothing about visibility).  What the plain declaration lines
cannot say is which of those counted names are actually visible outside the
module that defines them, and a citation needs to know: a paper may not quote
a name absent from the public API.

So the manifest also records visibility, without touching identity.  A row
for a `private` declaration is printed a second way: not among the plain
declaration lines, but in a trailing `private <module> <name>` section,
exactly mirroring the field section above.  A public declaration's line is
unchanged; module counts and the header total still cover both sections —
privatizing a declaration must not move it off the count, only off the
public section.

## The hypothesis section

A trailing `hypothesis <module> <theorem> <condition>` row names, for a
PUBLIC theorem only, one binder whose type's head constant is one of our own
Prop-valued definitions — a NAMED CONDITION.  It is the middle of three
levels, where axiom-level is covered twice over already (the per-capstone
`#print axioms` guards and the whole-library audit) and modeling-level
("time is unit-cost AST steps", "the damage model is the companion paper's")
stays prose and is never represented here.  **A theorem with no row in this
section is not a theorem that assumes nothing** — an anonymous hypothesis (a
bare equation, an upstream typeclass, an unnamed `∀`-statement) carries no
row by design, same as a theorem that is genuinely hypothesis-free.
The hypothesis ratchet reads this section as a delta ratchet, never an
absolute gate, on exactly this rule's own precedent (`visratchet`'s seed rule
was wrong four times before it stabilized).

The rule, mechanically. A binder's type is headed by a constant `C` — read
off `Expr.getAppFn`, so a bare `∀`/`Eq`/`≤`-shaped hypothesis has no head
constant and is skipped outright — where `C` compiles to `ConstantInfo.defnInfo`
whose own declared type, stripped of every one of its leading binders, is
exactly `Prop`; and the binder is applied at exactly that many arguments, so
a predicate used as data rather than asserted is not counted as a condition.
A `structure`/`class` compiles to an `inductive` and so is excluded when named
directly (the corpus authors none of its own Prop-valued today) — but one of
its FIELDS is itself a `defnInfo` projection and passes the same test as any
other definition when a hypothesis names it directly (`A.realizes n x` for
`A : Asm`, `RealizabilityCCC.Asm.realizes` being Prop-valued): a structure
does not shield its own named, Prop-valued fields from this rule, and none is
special-cased out.  `instImplicit` binders are skipped outright rather than
tested — the corpus authors no typeclass of its own (checked 2026-08-09), so
an instance argument is always an upstream witness (`Fintype`, `DecidableEq`,
an ergodic-theory hypothesis), never a modeling condition.  The walk does not
look inside a binder's own type: a conjunction bundling two conditions
(`Corrects U B c ∧ NonExpansive U c`) reads as one anonymous hypothesis, not
two rows — the design record's rule is stated at the binder, not recursively.

## The instance section

A trailing `instance <module> <theorem> <class>` row names, for a PUBLIC
theorem only, one CLASS an `instImplicit` binder of the theorem carries —
`Fintype`, `DecidableEq`, `MeasurableSpace`, and so on.  This is the
mechanical half of a per-binder classification a citing paper's FV row makes
by hand: whether a binder carries the paper's own mathematics, or is a Lean
encoding the paper has no counterpart for — a class instance, a decidability,
finiteness or measurability witness, a carrier's encoding.  This section only
lists WHICH classes a theorem's instance binders carry, so that
classification can be read off the manifest rather than re-derived by
re-opening the declaration; it classifies nothing itself — an instance
binder recorded here can still be genuinely load-bearing mathematics, not
merely an encoding artifact — and its coverage carries the same caution as
the hypothesis section above: a theorem absent here is not a theorem free of
instance binders, and a class recorded here is not thereby judged a mere
adapter.

The rule, mechanically.  Every `instImplicit` binder of a public theorem's
Pi-type is read off, in the same walk `theoremConditions` uses for the
hypothesis section but unfiltered by Prop-sortedness or package membership:
the corpus authors no typeclass of its own (see the hypothesis section
above), so every instance-implicit binder here names an upstream class, and
its head constant — read off `Expr.getAppFn`, exactly as a named condition's
head is — is what this section records.  Unlike a named condition, a
binder's arity is not checked: an instance-implicit binder's type is the
class fully applied by construction, never a partial application used as
data.
-/

open Lean

/-- Library roots of the package `alt`. -/
def altRoots : List Name := [`ALT]

/-- Library roots of the package `classical_upstream`, by field. -/
def classicalRoots : List Name :=
  [`CodeLayer, `AIT, `ChaitinIncompleteness, `Learning, `AnytimeValid, `ErgodicTheory,
   `Realizability, `ShannonInfo]

/-- Library roots of the package `elementary_cellular_automata`. -/
def automataRoots : List Name := [`Nonautonomous, `ECA]

/-- Default output path of a package (the manifests are consumed outside this workspace). -/
def defaultOutput (pkg : String) : System.FilePath :=
  ".." / "scripts" /
    (if pkg == "classical" then "classical-decls.txt"
     else if pkg == "automata" then "automata-decls.txt" else "alt-decls.txt")

/-- A module belongs to a package iff it is one of its library roots or lies under one. -/
def inRoots (roots : List Name) (m : Name) : Bool :=
  roots.any fun r => m == r || r.isPrefixOf m

/-- A module of any of the three packages: the test for "our own" Prop-valued definitions. -/
def isOurModule (m : Name) : Bool := inRoots (altRoots ++ classicalRoots ++ automataRoots) m

/-- Helper names Lean reserves for an inductive type or a constructor.  These are
dropped only when the parent really *is* an inductive or a constructor, which is
what makes a name-shaped test sound: for such a parent Lean owns these names, so
no authored declaration can occupy one.

Where the parent is anything else the name is fair game — `ALT.RecursorAlgebra`
authors a `def rec` (a bounded recursor, the point of the module), and a blanket
"drop anything called `rec`" rule silently ate it. -/
def inductiveHelpers : List String :=
  ["recOn", "casesOn", "below", "ibelow", "brecOn", "binductionOn",
   "noConfusion", "noConfusionType", "injEq", "inj", "sizeOf_spec", "sizeOf_inj",
   "ctorIdx", "toCtorIdx", "ofNat"]

/-- Helper names the equation compiler and the attribute machinery generate for a
definition.  Dropped when the parent is a declaration. -/
def definitionHelpers : List String :=
  ["eq_def", "eq_unfold", "induct", "fun_cases", "fun_cases_unfolding",
   "congr_simp", "splitter"]

/-- `numberedForm "match_" s` — a generated family indexed by one or more
numerals: `match_1`, and its splitters `match_1_1`, `match_1_2`, … -/
def numberedForm (prefix' s : String) : Bool :=
  s.startsWith prefix' &&
    let rest := s.drop prefix'.length
    !rest.isEmpty && rest.all fun c => c.isDigit || c == '_'

/-- The source-level name of a declaration: `private` mangles `foo` into
`_private.Mod.0.foo`, and it is the source-level name the counting rule reads. -/
def userName (n : Name) : Name := (privateToUserName? n).getD n

/-- Is `n` an inductive type or one of its constructors? -/
def isTypeLike (env : Environment) (n : Name) : Bool :=
  match env.find? n with
  | some (.inductInfo _) | some (.ctorInfo _) => true
  | _ => false

/-- The generated-name half of the counting rule: a helper name hangs off the
declaration it was generated for, so it is dropped only when that parent really
exists — as an inductive/constructor for `inductiveHelpers`, as any declaration
for `definitionHelpers`.  Without that witness the test is a guess about a name,
and guesses about names drop authored declarations.

The parent is sought under both the raw and the source-level name: a private
definition's matcher hangs off the mangled parent, while a *public* matcher's
equation lemmas are themselves private and hang off the unmangled one. -/
def isGeneratedName (env : Environment) (raw user : Name) : Bool :=
  match user with
  | .str _ s =>
      let parent (p : Name → Bool) := p raw.getPrefix || p user.getPrefix
      (parent (isTypeLike env ·) && inductiveHelpers.contains s)
        || (parent (env.contains ·)
              && (definitionHelpers.contains s
                    || numberedForm "eq_" s || numberedForm "match_" s
                    || numberedForm "proof_" s))
  | _ => true

/-- A structure projection `S.f` (including parent projections `S.toFoo`). -/
def isProjection (env : Environment) (n : Name) : Bool :=
  match n with
  | .str p f => isStructure env p && (getStructureFields env p).contains (Name.mkSimple f)
  | _ => false

/-- The counting rule: does this constant count as a declaration?  The
name-shaped tests read the source-level name, so that a `private` declaration is
counted while the helpers Lean generates *for* it still are not. -/
def counts (env : Environment) (n : Name) (ci : ConstantInfo) : Bool :=
  !((userName n).isInternal
    || isGeneratedName env n (userName n)
    || isAuxRecursor env n
    || isNoConfusion env n
    || (match ci with | .ctorInfo _ | .recInfo _ => true | _ => false)
    || isProjection env n)

/-- Is this constant a *field* — an authored structure projection?  These are the
citable-but-not-counted names of the field section: exactly the projections
`counts` excludes, minus anything internal or generated (a projection is neither,
but the rule states what it means rather than relying on that). -/
def isField (env : Environment) (n : Name) : Bool :=
  isProjection env n
    && !(userName n).isInternal
    && !isGeneratedName env n (userName n)

/-- Is `n` a name Lean's `private` mangling hides — the visibility half of the
manifest, orthogonal to the identity half `userName` already handles: a
`private` declaration is counted exactly like a public one (see `counts`),
and this only decides which section of the manifest its row lands in. -/
def isPrivateDecl (n : Name) : Bool := (privateToUserName? n).isSome

/-- Strip every leading `forallE` off `e`, returning what remains together
with how many binders were stripped — a declaration's own arity, read from
its compiled type rather than counted from source. -/
def stripAllForalls : Expr → Expr × Nat
  | .forallE _ _ body _ => let (r, k) := stripAllForalls body; (r, k + 1)
  | e => (e, 0)

/-- Is `e` exactly the sort `Prop` (`Sort 0`)? -/
def isPropSort : Expr → Bool
  | .sort .zero => true
  | _ => false

/-- Is `n` a NAMED CONDITION — see the module docstring's "hypothesis
section", which this implements.  Returns the arity a fully-applied
occurrence must match (not a `Bool`), so the caller can tell a saturated
assertion from a predicate used as data. -/
def namedConditionArity? (env : Environment) (n : Name) : Option Nat :=
  match env.find? n with
  | some (.defnInfo info) =>
      match env.getModuleIdxFor? n with
      | some idx =>
          match env.header.moduleNames[idx]? with
          | some m =>
              if isOurModule m then
                let (result, arity) := stripAllForalls info.type
                if isPropSort result then some arity else none
              else none
          | none => none
      | none => none
  | _ => none

/-- The named-condition name at the head of a binder's type `d`, if `d` is
EXACTLY one of our own Prop-valued definitions fully applied — matched
against that definition's own arity, so a partial application (the
definition used as data, not asserted) is correctly not a condition. -/
def namedConditionAt (env : Environment) (d : Expr) : Option Name :=
  match d.getAppFn.constName? with
  | some n =>
      match namedConditionArity? env n with
      | some arity => if d.getAppNumArgs == arity then some n else none
      | none => none
  | none => none

/-- Every named condition among the binders of a declaration's Pi-type `ty`
(a theorem's `ConstantInfo.type`) — see the module docstring's "hypothesis
section" for the full rule, including why `instImplicit` binders are skipped
outright and why the walk does not look inside a binder's own type. -/
def theoremConditions (env : Environment) : Expr → Array Name
  | .forallE _ d body bi =>
      let rest := theoremConditions env body
      if bi == .instImplicit then rest
      else
        match namedConditionAt env d with
        | some n => rest.push n
        | none => rest
  | _ => #[]

/-- Every CLASS carried by an `instImplicit` binder among the binders of a
declaration's Pi-type `ty` (a theorem's `ConstantInfo.type`) — see the module
docstring's "the instance section" for the full rule.  Unlike
`theoremConditions`, this is not scoped to the package: an instance binder's
class is Mathlib's or Foundation's by construction (the corpus authors no
typeclass of its own). -/
def theoremInstances : Expr → Array Name
  | .forallE _ d body bi =>
      let rest := theoremInstances body
      if bi == .instImplicit then
        match d.getAppFn.constName? with
        | some n => rest.push n
        | none => rest
      else rest
  | _ => #[]

/-- Every `(module, declaration)` pair of the package recorded in `env`, split
into the public pairs and the `private` pairs (see the module docstring's
"private section"); every `(module, field)` pair (citable, not counted — see
"the field section"); the package modules `env` knows about (a module
that declares nothing still counts as a module, and is emitted with a count
of 0); every `(module, theorem, condition)` triple a PUBLIC theorem's
binders carry (see "the hypothesis section"); and every `(module, theorem,
class)` triple a PUBLIC theorem's instance binders carry (see "the instance
section"). -/
def packagePairs (isPackageModule : Name → Bool) (env : Environment) :
    Array (Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name) × Array Name ×
      Array (Name × Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name × Name) := Id.run do
  let mods := env.header.moduleNames
  let data := env.header.moduleData
  let mut pubPairs := #[]
  let mut privPairs := #[]
  let mut fields := #[]
  let mut seen := #[]
  let mut hyps := #[]
  let mut insts := #[]
  for i in [0 : mods.size] do
    let m := mods[i]!
    if isPackageModule m then
      seen := seen.push m
      for n in data[i]!.constNames do
        if let some ci := env.find? n then
          if counts env n ci then
            if isPrivateDecl n then
              privPairs := privPairs.push (m, userName n)
            else
              pubPairs := pubPairs.push (m, userName n)
              if let .thmInfo _ := ci then
                for cond in theoremConditions env ci.type do
                  hyps := hyps.push (m, userName n, cond)
                for cls in theoremInstances ci.type do
                  insts := insts.push (m, userName n, cls)
          else if isField env n then
            fields := fields.push (m, userName n)
  return (pubPairs, privPairs, fields, seen, hyps, insts)

/-- Sort the lines and drop adjacent duplicates. -/
def sortedNub (xs : Array String) : Array String := Id.run do
  let sorted := xs.qsort (fun a b => decide (a < b))
  let mut out := #[]
  for x in sorted do
    if out.back? != some x then
      out := out.push x
  return out

/-- Import roots and list the package's `(module, declaration)` pairs.

`loadExts` is REQUIRED and its default is `false`: `isStructure`,
`isAuxRecursor` and `isNoConfusion` read environment extensions, and an
environment imported without them answers `false` to all three — the filter
would silently stop filtering rather than fail. -/
unsafe def scanRoots (isPackageModule : Name → Bool) (roots : Array Name) :
    IO (Array (Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name) × Array Name ×
      Array (Name × Name × Name) × Array (Name × Name × Name)) := do
  enableInitializersExecution
  let env ← importModules (roots.map ({ module := · })) {} (loadExts := true)
  return packagePairs isPackageModule env

unsafe def main (args : List String) : IO Unit := do
  initSearchPath (← findSysroot)

  let (pkg, rest) ← match args with
    | pkg :: rest =>
        if pkg == "alt" || pkg == "classical" || pkg == "automata" then pure (pkg, rest)
        else throw <| IO.userError s!"unknown package {pkg}: expected `alt`, `classical`, `automata`"
    | [] => throw <| IO.userError "usage: lake env lean --run DeclManifest.lean (alt|classical|automata) [OUT]"
  let roots :=
    if pkg == "classical" then classicalRoots
    else if pkg == "automata" then automataRoots else altRoots
  let out : System.FilePath :=
    match rest with
    | path :: _ => path
    | [] => defaultOutput pkg
  if let some dir := out.parent then
    unless ← dir.pathExists do
      throw <| IO.userError s!"output directory does not exist: {dir}\n\
        pass an output path: lake env lean --run DeclManifest.lean PKG OUT"

  let (pubPairs, privPairs, fields, mods, hyps, insts) ← scanRoots (inRoots roots) roots.toArray

  let pubDeclLines := sortedNub <| pubPairs.map fun (m, n) => s!"{m} {n}"
  let privDeclLines := sortedNub <| privPairs.map fun (m, n) => s!"{m} {n}"
  let fieldLines := sortedNub <| fields.map fun (m, n) => s!"{m} {n}"
  let modules := sortedNub <| mods.map toString
  let hypLines := sortedNub <| hyps.map fun (m, thm, cond) => s!"{m} {thm} {cond}"
  let instLines := sortedNub <| insts.map fun (m, thm, cls) => s!"{m} {thm} {cls}"

  -- The combined public+private set, kept purely for counting: module counts
  -- and the header total must not move when a declaration is privatized,
  -- only its section does (see the module docstring's "private section").
  -- Hypothesis and instance rows are NOT part of this count either — they
  -- are a citable fact about an already-counted theorem, not a fifth or
  -- sixth kind of declaration, and the header below is left untouched by
  -- them for the same reason.
  let declLines := sortedNub <| pubDeclLines ++ privDeclLines
  let counted : Array (String × Nat) := modules.map fun m =>
    (m, declLines.foldl (init := 0) fun acc l =>
      if l.startsWith s!"{m} " then acc + 1 else acc)

  let header :=
    s!"# generated by formal/DeclManifest.lean; modules: {modules.size}; \
       total decls: {declLines.size}; private: {privDeclLines.size}; \
       fields: {fieldLines.size} (structure projections: citable, NOT counted)"
  let moduleLines := counted.map fun (m, c) => s!"module {m} {c}"
  let fieldSection := fieldLines.map fun l => s!"field {l}"
  let privSection := privDeclLines.map fun l => s!"private {l}"
  let hypSection := hypLines.map fun l => s!"hypothesis {l}"
  let instSection := instLines.map fun l => s!"instance {l}"
  IO.FS.writeFile out <|
    String.intercalate "\n"
      (header :: (moduleLines ++ pubDeclLines ++ fieldSection ++ privSection
                    ++ hypSection ++ instSection).toList) ++ "\n"

  IO.eprintln s!"wrote {out}: {modules.size} modules, {declLines.size} declarations \
    ({privDeclLines.size} private), {fieldLines.size} fields, {hypLines.size} hypothesis rows, \
    {instLines.size} instance rows"
